Theory:
Nowadays Decision Tree algorithm is known by its modern name CART which stands for Classification and Regression Tree. Classification and Regression Tree or CART is a term introduced by Leo Breiman to refer to Decision Tree algorithm that can be used for classification and regression modeling problems.
The CART algorithm provides a formation for other important algorithms like bagged decision tree, random forest and boosted decision tree. In the kernel I will solve a classification problem so I will refer to the algorithm as a Decision Tree classification problem.

Steps of CART
1. Evaluate Best Split for Each Feature
   - Compute impurity or error
   - Check all features and their possible split values
   - Identify the split that gives the maximum reduction of error

2. Select the Optimal Split
   - Compare impurity decrease for all features
   - Choose the impurity feature and threshold pair with the best score
   - This becomes the splitting value for the current node

3. Create Binary Node Child
   - Split the dataset into Left Node  and Right Node 
   - Assign corresponding sample to each child node

4. Apply Recursive Splitting
   - Repeat the same process for each child node
   - Continue until a stopping condition is met

Diagram Structure (Decision Tree Hierarchy):
                  [Root Node]
                 /           \
       [Internal Node]   [Internal Node]
        /           \            \
[Internal Node]  [Leaf Node]  [Leaf Node]
  /         \
[Leaf Node] [Leaf Node]

Splitting Criteria for Classification:
Gini = 1 - ∑(i=1 to n) pi²

Where:
- pi = proportion of class
- n = number of classes
- Gini = 0: node is pure
- Gini close to 1: node has high impurity

